00:12:16,320 --> 00:12:17,880
I'm gonna miss my five o 'clock flight.

258
00:12:18,140 --> 00:12:20,220
Why? I don't even know.

259
00:12:20,240 --> 00:12:21,320
I don't even know why I'm here.

260
00:12:21,400 --> 00:12:22,760
Jason, just relax.

261
00:12:22,940 --> 00:12:23,740
Enjoy the time off.

262
00:12:23,800 --> 00:12:24,980
You work too hard as it is.

263
00:12:25,240 --> 00:12:27,620
It's easy for you to say your whole world's in order.

264
00:12:27,920 --> 00:12:31,020
No, I just say that because I'm fixing Dolph a sandwich.

265
00:12:31,520 --> 00:12:33,700
Maggie, I don't want mustard on my dinner.

266
00:12:47,380 --> 00:12:49,820
I love you too, sweetheart.

267
00:12:51,620 --> 00:12:52,600
I love you.

268
00:12:54,580 --> 00:12:56,100
I love you.

269
00:12:56,100 --> 00:12:56,480
I love you.

270
00:12:56,480 --> 00:12:57,440
I love you.

271
00:12:57,440 --> 00:12:57,600
I love you.

272
00:12:57,600 --> 00:12:58,340
I love you.

273
00:12:58,340 --> 00:12:58,740
I love you.

274
00:12:58,740 --> 00:12:58,980
I love you.

275
00:12:58,980 --> 00:12:59,160
I love you.

276
00:12:59,160 --> 00:12:59,660
I love you.

277
00:13:01,740 --> 00:13:03,140
I love you.

278
00:13:03,620 --> 00:13:07,120
Dad, I'm telling you, I just saw this beer, a big one with alers up to here.

279
00:13:07,280 --> 00:13:11,240
Heh. You know, it's daylight.

280
00:13:11,400 --> 00:13:12,200
Shouldn't you be sleeping?

281
00:13:12,480 --> 00:13:13,020
Dad, it's weird.

282
00:13:13,020 --> 00:13:14,040
I'm not even tired here.

283
00:13:14,220 --> 00:13:17,720
You know, I mean, if Uncle George had only put in a satellite dish, this would be a

284
00:13:17,720 --> 00:13:18,660
primo place to live.

285
00:13:19,800 --> 00:13:20,880
Look, I got some calls.

286
00:13:21,000 --> 00:13:21,680
I'm sorry I'm busy.

287
00:13:21,880 --> 00:13:23,740
Hey, come on, forget about your lists, all right?

288
00:13:24,020 --> 00:13:26,360
What do you say we go kill something and eat it, huh?

289
00:13:29,060 --> 00:13:32,700
Excuse me. I'm looking for...

290
00:13:32,700 --> 00:13:34,260
you. Who? Me?

291
00:13:35,220 --> 00:13:36,060
Have we met?

292
00:13:36,540 --> 00:13:37,400
In a way.

293
00:13:37,540 --> 00:13:39,160
Your picture's in the bedroom.

294
00:13:40,220 --> 00:13:44,920
It's an old picture, but I'd know you with or without those sideburns.

295
00:13:46,360 --> 00:13:47,880
And you are?

296
00:13:48,420 --> 00:13:51,340
Oh, I'm sorry, I'm Nancy Ashton.

297
00:13:51,680 --> 00:13:55,760
I was your uncle George's friend.

298
00:13:56,040 --> 00:14:01,760
Friend? Those three years we had together were the happiest years I've ever had.

299
00:14:01,760 --> 00:14:03,340
Oh, I -I'm sorry.

300
00:14:04,280 --> 00:14:06,480
I promised myself I -I wouldn't do this.

301
00:14:06,640 --> 00:14:14,320
It's okay. George was -was the kindest, most caring, most wonderful man in the

302
00:14:14,320 --> 00:14:16,600
world. But -but you know that.

303
00:14:16,920 --> 00:14:18,920
I mean, you were his favorite nephew.

304
00:14:20,940 --> 00:14:23,200
I was? I'll show you.

305
00:14:26,360 --> 00:14:27,860
Dad, I think I got this figured out.

306
00:14:27,960 --> 00:14:30,320
We're at the wrong dead Uncle George's cabin.

307
00:14:31,280 --> 00:14:33,760
Mike, I brought you here to carry things, not to think.

308
00:14:34,660 --> 00:14:35,040
Dad, come on.

309
00:14:35,280 --> 00:14:37,040
I mean, look at this wonderful guy she's talking about.

310
00:14:37,120 --> 00:14:39,480
He'd be the same rotten, selfish guy who deserted his wife.

311
00:14:39,940 --> 00:14:42,480
Here. What are these?

312
00:14:42,940 --> 00:14:46,240
Oh, every Christmas card you sent him for the past 15 years.

313
00:14:47,140 --> 00:14:48,880
Oh, I had no idea.

314
00:14:49,920 --> 00:14:51,080
Oh, I'm sorry.

315
00:14:51,080 --> 00:14:52,040
I forgot why I came.

316
00:14:52,140 --> 00:14:53,220
I brought some food.

317
00:14:53,380 --> 00:14:54,200
It's in the car.

318
00:14:56,640 --> 00:14:59,800
Dad, I thought that Mom didn't send him any Christmas cards.

319
00:14:59,800 --> 00:15:02,020
But she didn't.

320
00:15:08,160 --> 00:15:09,940
["The Star -Spangled Banner"] All right, let's get down to business.

321
00:15:10,540 --> 00:15:14,000
Did everybody say hi to Uncle George's favorite nephew, Jason Seaver?

322
00:15:14,660 --> 00:15:18,440
Hey, Jason! Hey, Jason!

323
00:15:18,440 --> 00:15:21,800
Okay, now I'm gonna read this just the way he wrote it.

324
00:15:23,760 --> 00:15:27,960
I, George Washington Bowman, being of sound mind, at least I always thought so,

325
00:15:30,140 --> 00:15:32,380
Leave this my last will and testament.

326
00:15:33,520 --> 00:15:35,980
Now, I wanted to leave everything to my beloved Nancy.

327
00:15:36,260 --> 00:15:38,340
No! Sit down, Nance.

328
00:15:38,380 --> 00:15:39,620
I'm gonna honor your wishes.

329
00:15:40,500 --> 00:15:41,940
Thanks for the wonderful years.

330
00:15:42,320 --> 00:15:43,140
Isn't she pretty?

331
00:15:45,940 --> 00:15:47,900
I read that, but I have to agree.

332
00:15:49,220 --> 00:15:53,460
To my beloved nephew Jason Siever, I leave the cabin and the three or four acres.

333
00:15:53,460 --> 00:15:57,900
Claver never made it clear exactly what he sold me, the lying thief.

334
00:15:59,560 --> 00:16:02,020
Well, I read that, and I have to read with that, too.

335
00:16:03,960 --> 00:16:07,300
Jason, you're the one member of my family who didn't laugh when I said I was moving

336
00:16:07,300 --> 00:16:10,740
out here. In fact, I'll never forget what you told me.

337
00:16:10,980 --> 00:16:12,140
I'm sure you won't either.

338
00:16:12,640 --> 00:16:13,660
What'd you tell him?

339
00:16:13,920 --> 00:16:18,300
I forget. And to old family and friends in New York,

340
00:16:19,140 --> 00:16:21,260
I'd like to leave you with the following thoughts.

341
00:16:21,980 --> 00:16:24,640
I don't regret my decision to leave New York.

342
00:16:25,500 --> 00:16:27,740
I don't regret my decision to follow a dream.

343
00:16:29,100 --> 00:16:34,140
I wish people had understood not everybody's cut out for a life that is planned and

344
00:16:34,140 --> 00:16:35,560
regulated as monthly house payments.

345
00:16:36,580 --> 00:16:37,800
Sorry about that.

346
00:16:38,520 --> 00:16:40,120
But I bear no one ill will.

347
00:16:41,060 --> 00:16:45,500
In fact, I hope everyone finds the joy and contentment I found over these years,

348
00:16:45,680 --> 00:16:49,060
especially Harriet, who had the misfortune to marry me.

349
00:16:49,920 --> 00:16:52,540
So put away your handkerchiefs and enjoy yourselves.

350
00:16:53,000 --> 00:16:53,540
It's a funeral.

351
00:16:53,780 --> 00:16:54,300
It's a party.

352
00:16:54,940 --> 00:16:56,160
Stop! You're both right!

353
00:16:59,400 --> 00:17:00,600
Dad, what did you tell him?

354
00:17:00,720 --> 00:17:01,780
I have no idea.

355
00:17:01,940 --> 00:17:02,700
Come on, think, Dad.

356
00:17:02,700 --> 00:17:04,780
It must have been something meaningful and important.

357
00:17:04,940 --> 00:17:07,160
I mean, it's not like you say important, meaningful things that often.

358
00:17:22,140 --> 00:17:24,500
Oh, sorry. I didn't realize anybody else was out here.

359
00:17:24,600 --> 00:17:25,660
Oh, that's all right.

360
00:17:25,720 --> 00:17:26,580
You can stay.

361
00:17:28,860 --> 00:17:34,080
I know that George would want to sit in there having fun, but that's a little tough.

362
00:17:34,400 --> 00:17:40,060
Yes, yeah. Nancy, I'm a little embarrassed to even ask, but do you have any idea

363
00:17:40,060 --> 00:17:42,220
what I might have said that meant so much to George?

364
00:17:42,840 --> 00:17:43,940
You don't know?

365
00:17:45,720 --> 00:17:47,520
Oh, you really don't know?

366
00:17:48,700 --> 00:17:50,800
Well, you told him that...

367
00:17:50,800 --> 00:17:52,740
Oh, the mincemeat's marvelous.

368
00:17:53,420 --> 00:17:54,100
Thank you, Innova.

369
00:17:54,480 --> 00:17:57,800
I was just telling your dad what he told your Uncle George.

370
00:17:58,220 --> 00:18:00,620
Oh, oh, gosh, well, I'd love to hear it, too.

371
00:18:01,200 --> 00:18:07,100
Well, you said to him, what's your life without your dreams?

372
00:18:08,880 --> 00:18:10,220
This man said this?

373
00:18:11,880 --> 00:18:14,160
You sure it wasn't, what's your life without your lists?

374
00:18:15,140 --> 00:18:23,340
I'm sure. You also said that staying open to possibilities is so much more important

375
00:18:23,340 --> 00:18:27,000
than planning every move or watching every dime.

376
00:18:29,480 --> 00:18:30,560
I had forgot.

377
00:18:32,160 --> 00:18:34,220
I got the right cabin, just the wrong father.

378
00:18:36,640 --> 00:18:37,860
Nancy, everything all right?

379
00:18:38,880 --> 00:18:40,760
Mm -hmm. Yeah, yeah, yeah.

380
00:18:40,760 --> 00:18:41,420
No, I'm fine.

381
00:18:41,620 --> 00:18:42,420
Yeah. Go on in.

382
00:18:42,420 --> 00:18:43,080
Have a good party.

383
00:18:43,580 --> 00:18:47,880
Enjoy. Excuse me.

384
00:18:48,240 --> 00:18:53,580
Jason, as your Uncle George's attorney and friend, I want to thank you for making

385
00:18:53,580 --> 00:18:58,040
the trip. As your cab driver, I want to remind you I'll be picking you up at 6 AM

386
00:18:58,040 --> 00:19:03,720
tomorrow. Too bad you couldn't stick around a couple more days and help finish the

387
00:19:03,720 --> 00:19:05,140
cabin. What are you talking about?

388
00:19:06,320 --> 00:19:10,560
Well, George was just fixing to lay up the chimney top when he passed away.

389
00:19:11,580 --> 00:19:14,100
But as your real estate broker, it won't affect the price.

390
00:19:14,500 --> 00:19:19,760
Good. As a chiropractor, you shouldn't hunch over like that.

391
00:19:20,580 --> 00:19:26,520
I'm sorry. Hey, Dad, were you really like that lady, Seth?

392
00:19:26,720 --> 00:19:29,460
Ah. Eh, long time ago.

393
00:19:30,100 --> 00:19:32,640
You mean, so you weren't always so...

394
00:19:32,640 --> 00:19:37,980
Anal? Okay. Mike, there was a time I didn't even make any lists.

395
00:19:38,160 --> 00:19:38,700
I'll get out of here.

396
00:19:38,700 --> 00:19:39,380
No, it's true.

397
00:19:39,500 --> 00:19:42,900
You know, your mama and I, one summer, we hitchhiked right across this country.

398
00:19:43,800 --> 00:19:44,920
How'd she do her hair?

399
00:19:45,300 --> 00:19:47,820
Your mom was a lot looser then, too.

400
00:19:48,760 --> 00:19:51,260
We just camped out under the stars, just like this.

401
00:19:52,040 --> 00:19:55,160
Yeah? So when'd you start going downhill?

402
00:19:57,300 --> 00:19:58,920
Soon after you were born.

403
00:20:00,340 --> 00:20:01,360
That's all my fault.

404
00:20:02,720 --> 00:20:06,740
Well, once you have kids, I guess it's tough to be one.

405
00:20:10,580 --> 00:20:11,780
Look at this, Mike.

406
00:20:11,900 --> 00:20:16,760
Wow. Imagine living out here, breathing this air, and seeing these stars every

407
00:20:16,760 --> 00:20:21,860
night. Dad, I mean, look, you own this place.

408
00:20:21,940 --> 00:20:23,120
I mean, you could do whatever you want.

409
00:20:23,960 --> 00:20:24,980
Gotta be realistic.

410
00:20:25,560 --> 00:20:26,200
Oh, come on, Dad.

411
00:20:26,200 --> 00:20:29,260
What is not realistic about you and Mom spending a little time out here?

412
00:20:29,300 --> 00:20:31,880
Yeah, right. See your mother out here?

413
00:20:31,900 --> 00:20:33,040
Hi, Maggie. I'm home.

414
00:20:33,280 --> 00:20:34,720
Could you gut the moose, babe?

415
00:20:36,720 --> 00:20:40,940
Yeah, I just mean that, I don't know, maybe you shouldn't sell this place.

416
00:20:41,660 --> 00:20:44,140
Mike, my life is in New York.

417
00:20:44,700 --> 00:20:46,200
But end of conversation.

418
00:20:50,080 --> 00:20:53,640
I just think it's too bad you don't have someone talking to you like that neat guy

419
00:20:53,640 --> 00:20:54,500
Uncle George did.

420
00:20:57,520 --> 00:21:00,400
We have the same stars in New York, you know.

421
00:21:02,560 --> 00:21:04,400
You just can't see him.

422
00:21:16,780 --> 00:21:17,880
Sky is falling.

423
00:21:18,440 --> 00:21:20,440
Sorry, Mike. Dad?

424
00:21:20,720 --> 00:21:22,000
Up here. Come on out.

425
00:21:22,320 --> 00:21:24,200
No, thanks. I'd rather sleep.

426
00:21:24,600 --> 00:21:26,640
You come up or I'll drop another rock.

427
00:21:31,500 --> 00:21:33,300
All right, I'm coming, I'm coming.

428
00:21:35,520 --> 00:21:37,140
Dad, what the heck are you doing?

429
00:21:37,420 --> 00:21:41,860
Hey, Mike. Come on, you act like you've never seen your dad do masonry at sunrise

430
00:21:41,860 --> 00:21:44,280
before. Huh? Come on, get up here.

431
00:21:44,280 --> 00:21:44,760
Give me your hand.

432
00:21:44,960 --> 00:21:47,620
Dad, we've got to be in the airport in less than an hour.

433
00:21:48,120 --> 00:21:50,320
Well, it'll just take a little longer than that, Mike.

434
00:21:50,320 --> 00:21:50,980
I don't want to hurry.

435
00:21:50,980 --> 00:21:51,880
I want to get this right.

436
00:21:52,300 --> 00:21:53,640
But, but the plane.

437
00:21:54,040 --> 00:21:55,720
Mike, there will be other flights.

438
00:21:56,180 --> 00:21:56,820
Come on, give me a rough.

439
00:21:57,800 --> 00:22:00,320
Dad, did you by chance fall off the roof and land on your head?

440
00:22:00,320 --> 00:22:02,860
Oh, no. I just started thinking last night.

441
00:22:02,980 --> 00:22:06,240
I think it would be nice to have a finished cabin by the time your mother comes up

442
00:22:06,240 --> 00:22:07,640
here. When's she coming up?

443
00:22:07,660 --> 00:22:08,160
I don't know.

444
00:22:08,160 --> 00:22:08,580
I don't know.

445
00:22:08,580 --> 00:22:09,540
Maybe a vacation.

446
00:22:09,780 --> 00:22:11,360
I haven't made a plan yet.

447
00:22:11,720 --> 00:22:13,420
But, Dad, I thought you were selling this place.

448
00:22:14,100 --> 00:22:16,280
Yeah, well, you also thought I was cheap.

449
00:22:18,020 --> 00:22:20,060
Jason Siever. He's an adult.

450
00:22:20,400 --> 00:22:21,320
He's a dreamer.

451
00:22:21,600 --> 00:22:22,960
Stop. You're both lying.

452
00:22:25,140 --> 00:22:28,960
Hey. I can't believe this view up here.

453
00:22:28,960 --> 00:22:30,760
All right, have you looked around?

454
00:22:36,740 --> 00:22:38,260
We'll be back in a moment.
